<?xml version='1.0' encoding='UTF-8'?>
<section xml:id="two-sample-t-tests">
  <title>Inference for Two Means</title>
  
  <subsection xml:id="ts-design">
    <title>Identify the Sampling Design</title>

    <definition xml:id="ts-def-dep-samples">
      <title>Dependent Samples</title>
      <statement>
        <p> </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>

    <definition xml:id="ts-def-indep-samples">
      <title>Independent Samples</title>
      <statement>
        <p> </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>

    <example xml:id="ts-ex-id-design">
      <title>Choose the Appropriate Design</title>

      <statement>
        <p>For each study, identify whether the samples are dependent or independent. Identify, when appropriate, the pairing.</p>
        <p>
          <ol marker="a)">
            <li>
              <p>Blood glucose is measured before and after treatment in the same 30 patients.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>Twenty-four mice are randomly assigned to a supplement and a different 24 mice are assigned to a control diet.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>For each of 15 plants, one leaf is randomly assigned a spray and a matched leaf from the same plant is left untreated. The response is leaf damage.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>Researchers separately sample 18 fish from one large lake and 18 fish from another large lake.</p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </example>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="ts-paired">
    <title>Dependent Samples: Paired t-Test</title>

    <assemblage xml:id="ts-paired-req">
      <title>Requirements for Paired t-Test</title>
      <p>
        <ol marker="1)">
          <li>
            <p>The response is quantitative, and the target parameter is a population mean <m>\mu</m>. The population standard deviation <m>\sigma</m> is unknown.</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              The data must be paired and the pairs should be independent from each other. 
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>The observations come from an appropriate random sample. When sampling without replacement, the sample should be no more than about 10% of the population.</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>For a small sample, the differences should be roughly normal with no serious outliers. For a larger sample, inference is more tolerant of moderate nonnormality, but strong skewness and influential outliers still need attention.</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>Use a histogram or normal Q-Q plot to inspect raw data. A Shapiro-Wilk result may supplement the plots; a large p-value does not establish normality.</p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </assemblage>

    <assemblage xml:id="ts-paired-formula">
      <title>Paired t-Test</title>
      <p>Choose an order and define one difference for each complete pair, <m>d_i=x_i-y_i</m>. Let <m>\bar d</m> and <m>s_d</m> are the sample mean and sample standard deviation of the differences. If the hypothesized mean difference is <m>\mu_d</m>, then</p>
      <p>
        <md number="no">
          <mrow>H_0:\mu=\mu_d,\qquad t=\dfrac{\bar d- \mu_d}{\dfrac{s_d}{\sqrt n}},\qquad df=n-1.</mrow>
        </md>
      </p>
      <p>Here <m>n</m> is the number of complete pairs. Usually <m>\mu_d =0</m>. The alternative is <m>\mu &lt;\mu_d</m>, <m>\mu &gt;\mu_d</m>, or <m>\mu \ne\mu_d</m>.</p>
    </assemblage>
    
    <program language="r">
# x and y must be in the same participant order.
d = x - y
hist(d)
qqnorm(d)
qqline(d)

# Two equivalent ways to perform a two-sided paired test:
t.test(x, y, paired = TRUE, mu = 0, alternative = "two.sided")
t.test(d, mu = 0, alternative = "two.sided")
</program>

    <p>With summary statistics for the differences, calculate <m>t</m> from the formula and use <c>pt()</c>. With raw paired data, <c>t.test(x, y, paired = TRUE)</c> uses the differences <c>x - y</c>. Use <c>"greater"</c> or <c>"less"</c> when that matches the alternative. If a measurement is missing, omit the entire incomplete pair and count the remaining pairs; never remove missing values separately and then pair the shortened columns.</p>
    <p>For each test example, define the parameter and subtraction order, state the hypotheses, identify where the claim belongs, check the requirements, find the test statistic, degrees of freedom, and p-value, make the decision, and conclude in context.</p>

    <example xml:id="ts-paired-summary">
      <title>Blood-Pressure Reduction</title>
      <statement>
        <p>A random sample of 16 eligible adults participates in a program. Each adults systolic blood pressure is measured before and after the program. Let <m>d=\text{before}-\text{after}</m>. The differences have mean 5.2 mmHg and sample standard deviation 6.4 mmHg. Assume the population of differences is approximately normal. At <m>\alpha=0.05</m>, test the claim that the mean reduction is positive.</p>
      </statement>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="ts-paired-raw">
      <title>Two Measurement Devices</title>
      <statement>
        <p>Two devices measure systolic blood pressure on each of 10 randomly selected adults. The measurement order is randomized within each adult. A manufacturer claims that the two devices have the same population mean reading. Define differences as Device A minus Device B. At <m>\alpha=0.05</m>, is there sufficient evidence to reject the claim?</p>
        <program language="r">
device_a = c(128, 135, 142, 126, 151, 137, 133, 145, 139, 130)
device_b = c(129, 134, 141, 128, 149, 138, 132, 146, 141, 131)
</program>
      </statement>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="ts-paired-left">
      <title>Matched Root Samples: A Directional Claim</title>
      <statement>
        <p>Researchers select 20 independent plants. Within each plant, two comparable root samples are randomly assigned to a treatment or control condition. Define <m>d=\text{treated root length}-\text{control root length}</m>. The mean difference is <m>-0.8</m> millimeters and the sample standard deviation of the differences is 2.4 millimeters. At <m>\alpha=0.05</m>, do the results support the claim that the treatment decreases mean root length?</p>
      </statement>
    </example>
  </subsection>
  
  <subsection>
    <title>Independent Samples: Welch's t-Test</title>

    <assemblage>
      <title>Requirements for Two-Sample t-Test</title>
      <p>
        <ol marker="1)">
          <li>
            <p>The response is quantitative. Observations are independent within each sample and between the groups, with appropriate random sampling or random assignment.</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>For small samples, each population should be approximately normal without serious outliers. Examine each group separately. Larger samples allow more tolerance for moderate nonnormality.</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>Welch's procedure does not assume equal population variances.</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>Label Groups 1 and 2 explicitly. The sign of the statistic and the direction of a one-sided test depend on that order.</p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </assemblage>

    <assemblage xml:id="ts-ind-t-test">
      <title>Welch's Independent Two-Sample t-Test</title>
      <p>
        <md>
         t=\dfrac{(\bar x_1-\bar x_2)-\Delta_0}{\sqrt{\dfrac{s_1^2}{n_1}+\dfrac{s_2^2}{n_2}}}
        </md>
      </p>
      <p>The degrees of freedom are</p>
      <p>
        <md>
          \rm{df}=\dfrac{\left(\dfrac{s_1^2}{n_1}+\dfrac{s_2^2}{n_2}\right)^2}{\dfrac{(s_1^2/n_1)^2}{n_1-1}+\dfrac{(s_2^2/n_2)^2}{n_2-1}}
        </md>
      </p>
      <p>We will approximate df when doing calculations by hand with</p>
      <p>
        <md>
          \rm{df} = \min \left( n_1 - 1, n_2 - 1 \right)
        </md>
      </p>
    </assemblage>
    
    <p>For raw observations, use the vector order <c>group1, group2</c>. The option <c>var.equal = FALSE</c> selects Welchss method and is also Rss default.</p>
    <program language="r">
t.test(group1, group2, mu = 0, alternative = "two.sided", var.equal = FALSE)
    </program>

    <example xml:id="ts-math-test">
      <title>Math Test</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          The results of a state mathematics test for random samples of students taught by two different teachers at the same school are shown in the table below. Can you conclude that there is a difference in the mean mathematics test scores for the students of the two teachers? Use <m>\alpha = 0.10</m> and assume that the populations are normally distributed and the population variances are not equal.
        </p>
        
        <tabular top="minor" left="minor">
            <col halign="center" right="medium"/>
            <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
            <row header="yes" bottom="medium">
                <cell>Teacher 1</cell>
                <cell>Teacher 2</cell>
            </row>

            <row bottom="minor">
                <cell><m>\bar{x}_{1}=473</m></cell>
                <cell><m>\bar{x}_{2}=459</m></cell>
            </row>

            <row bottom="minor">
                <cell><m>s_{1}=39.7</m></cell>
                <cell><m>s_{2}=24.5</m></cell>
            </row>

            <row bottom="minor">
                <cell><m>n_{1}=8</m></cell>
                <cell><m>n_{2}=18</m></cell>
            </row>
        </tabular>
      </statement>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="ts-engineer">
      <title>Engineering</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
            An engineer wants to compare the tensile strengths of steel bars that are
            produced using a conventional method and an experimental method.
            To do so, the engineer randomly selects steel bars that are manufactured
            using each method and records the tensile strength (in Newtons per square
            mm) listed below
        </p>

        <p>
            Experimental Method:
            <md>
                <mrow>395 \quad 389 \quad 421 \quad 394 \quad 407 \quad 411 \quad 389 \quad 402 \quad 422 \quad 416 \quad 402 \quad 408 \quad 400 </mrow>
                <mrow>386 \quad 411 \quad 405 \quad 389 \quad 410 </mrow>
            </md>
        </p>

        <p>
            Conventional Method:
            <md>
                <mrow>362 \quad 352 \quad 380 \quad 382 \quad 413 \quad 384 \quad 400 \quad 378 \quad 419 \quad 379 \quad 384 \quad 388 </mrow>
                <mrow>372 \quad 383 </mrow>
            </md>
        </p>

        <p>
            At <m>\alpha = 0.05</m>, can the engineer support the claim that the
            experimental method produces steel with a greater mean tensile strength?
            Assume that the populations are normally distributed and that the population
            variances are not equal.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="ts-marine">
      <title>Marine Biologist</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          A marine researcher claims that the stomachs of blue crabs from one location
          contain less fish than the stomachs of blue crabs from another location.
          The stomach contents of a sample of 35 blue crabs from Location A contain a
          mean of 280 milligrams of fish and a standard deviation of 80 milligrams.
          The stomach contents of a sample of 30 blue crabs from Location B contain a
          mean of 320 milligrams of fish and a standard deviation of 60 milligrams.
          At <m>\alpha = 0.01</m> can you support the marine researcher's claim?
          Assume the population variances are not equal.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </example>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="ts-large-data">
    <title>A Larger Health Data Set</title>
    <example xml:id="ts-large-study">
      <title>Large Data Example: Changes Within and Between Groups</title>
      <statement>
        <p>The supplied file contains simulated data for 360 adults. In the hypothetical design, a simple random sample is selected from a large population of eligible adults, and participants are randomly assigned to Control, Walking, or Combined programs, with 120 in each group. Control receives usual information, Walking adds a walking program, and Combined adds walking plus nutrition counseling. Systolic blood pressure is recorded before assignment and after eight weeks. Each row is one participant; both measurements are in millimeters of mercury.</p>
        
        <p>Define <c>reduction = baseline_sbp - week8_sbp</c>, so a positive value means blood pressure decreased. Use <m>\alpha=0.05</m> for both tests below.</p>
        <p>
          <ol marker="a)">
            <li>
              <p>Select the Walking participants and examine the distribution of their reductions.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>Use a paired t-test to assess the claim that the Walking group has a positive population mean reduction.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>Compare reductions for the Combined and Walking groups using Welch's independent two-sample test. A researcher claims that the Combined program has a larger mean reduction. Use Combined minus Walking and state the full test conclusion.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>Explain why part (b) is paired but part (c) is independent, even though both analyses use before-and-after measurements.</p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </example>
  </subsection>
</section>
